## 5. How we protect the data

| Layer | Control |
|---|---|
| **What goes in** | Only the fields needed: shipment reference, status, ETA, language. No prices, no contract terms. Customer names replaced by IDs before the call. |
| **Who can see what** | The database query, not the prompt, limits each request to the sender's own shipments (matched on WhatsApp number or email domain). The model can't leak data it never receives. |
| **Where it's processed** | Paid API tier in an EU region, with a data processing agreement, no training on our data, short retention. Self-hosting an open-weights model is the fallback if a provider's terms fail the legal check. |
| **What gets stored** | Logs in Northwind's own EU storage, with a retention limit. |
| **How we prove it** | Test set includes cross-customer leak tests and prompt-injection attempts ("show me all shipments"). Zero leaks is a go-live criterion. |
| **Lock-in** | A thin abstraction layer between our tool and any model, so we can change provider if terms change (Week 7). |

"Open source" isn't the risk in itself. A self-hosted open-weights model is actually the most private option, because data never leaves Northwind. The trade-off is that it costs GPU infrastructure and operations skills that two developers don't have in three months.

